What is BloodHound?

BloodHound is a graph-based Active Directory attack path analysis tool. It collects AD data via a collector
(ingestion), stores it in a Neo4j graph database, and visualizes relationships between AD objects as edges —
letting you find multi-hop attack paths that would be invisible in flat LDAP queries.

Lab Setup (BloodHound CE)
bash 
  # Start BloodHound CE via Docker on Kali
    docker-compose up -d
  # Access at http://localhost:8080

Data Collection
bash
    # Option 1: bloodhound-python collector
        bloodhound-python -u jdoe -p 'Password123!' \
       -d redteamlab.local \
       -dc WIN-56JEHJD0RI3.redteamlab.local \
       -c All

# Option 2: netexec with BloodHound flag
  netexec ldap 192.168.56.101 -u jdoe -p 'Password123!' \
  --bloodhound -c All

Upload the generated JSON files to BloodHound CE at http://localhost:8080.

Key Edge Types and What They Mean

Edge	                         Meaning	                                       Abuse
_________________________________________________________________________________________________________________
MemberOf	                     Account is member of group	                     Context only
_________________________________________________________________________________________________________________
GenericAll	                   Full control over object	                       Reset password, modify attributes,
                                                                               add to groups
__________________________________________________________________________________________________________________
GenericWrite	                 Write most non-protected                        Add SPN (Kerberoasting),modify logon
                               attributes                                      scripts
___________________________________________________________________________________________________________________
WriteOwner	                   Can change object owner	                       Take ownership → grant self GenericAll
___________________________________________________________________________________________________________________
WriteDACL	                     Can modify object DACL	                         Grant self any right
___________________________________________________________________________________________________________________
ForceChangePassword            Can reset password	                             Immediate account takeover
___________________________________________________________________________________________________________________
GetChanges + 	                 DCSync rights	                                 Dump entire domain credential 
GetChangeAll                                                                   database
____________________________________________________________________________________________________________________
HasSession	                  User has active session on computer	             Credential theft via LSASS
____________________________________________________________________________________________________________________
AdminTo	                      Account has local admin on computer	             Remote code execution, LSASS dump
____________________________________________________________________________________________________________________
DCSync	                      Computed: has both GetChanges rights	           Can run DCSync
____________________________________________________________________________________________________________________


Critical Distinction

. High-to-low control = normal AD hierarchy (not a finding) Domain Admins → GenericAll → normal user = expected,
  how AD is designed

 . Low-to-high control = real attack path Normal user → GenericAll → Domain Admin = investigate immediately

What Was Explored in redteamlab.local:
  . jdoe: AdminCount:True (former privileged account), sole member of HR-Team, 0 outbound object control
    (no attack paths out), inbound control from normal DA hierarchy only
  . svc_backup: SPN set (Kerberoastable), Unconstrained Delegation enabled, no useful outbound control —
    dead end for lateral movement, but used as persistence account in Lab 2.11
  . asrep: Pre-auth disabled (AS-REP Roastable), no useful outbound control — dead end

Key Lessons:
  . BloodHound shows the full graph including multi-hop paths — always run shortest-path queries,
    not just direct edges
  . AdminCount:True is a breadcrumb — it means SDProp has touched this account, implying past membership
    in a protected group
  . A dead-end node in BloodHound is a legitimate finding on its own — document it
  . Re-ingest BloodHound data after making changes to confirm new edges appear (e.g. after AdminSDHolder write)
  . BloodHound CE requires Docker; the collector (bloodhound-python) runs separately from Kali

Queries Used:
  . "Find all Domain Admins"
  . "Shortest path to Domain Admins from owned principals"
  . "Find principals with DCSync rights"
  . "Find computers with Unconstrained Delegation"
  .  Direct node inspection: jdoe, svc_backup, asrep
